-- title:
--   Chart of a representing Grassmannian scheme is generated by coordinates
-- statement:
--   Fix a commutative ring $R$, an $R$-module $M$ and $k \in \mathbb{N}$. Let $Gr$ be a scheme (in the zeroth universe) with a morphism $p : Gr \to \operatorname{Spec} R$, and suppose given, for every commutative $R$-algebra $A$, a bijection $\mathrm{pt}_A$ between `Module.Grassmannian A (A ⊗[R] M) k`, the set of $A$-submodules $N \subseteq A \otimes_R M$ of the kind classified by that Mathlib notion, and the set of morphisms $g : \operatorname{Spec} A \to Gr$ with $g$ followed by $p$ equal to the structure morphism $\operatorname{Spec} A \to \operatorname{Spec} R$; the hypothesis `hpt` asserts naturality: for every $R$-algebra map $\varphi : A \to B$ and every $N$ over $A$, the morphism attached to the base change `Module.Grassmannian.map φ N` is $\operatorname{Spec} \varphi$ followed by $\mathrm{pt}_A(N)$. Let $V$ assign to each $k$-tuple $x : \mathrm{Fin}\,k \to M$ an open subscheme $V_x$ of $Gr$ such that, for all $A$ and all $N$, the set-theoretic range of $\mathrm{pt}_A(N)$ lies in $V_x$ exactly when $v \mapsto \sum_i v_i \cdot [1 \otimes x_i]$ is a bijection $A^k \to (A \otimes_R M)/N$. Let $g : \iota \to M$ have $R$-span all of $M$, fix $x$, and let $S$ be a commutative $R$-algebra with $N \in$ `Module.Grassmannian S (S ⊗[R] M) k` whose morphism $\mathrm{pt}_S(N)$ is an open immersion with open range exactly $V_x$. Then the $R$-subalgebra of $S$ generated by the set of all $c_i$, where $a$ ranges over $\iota$, $c \in S^k$ satisfies $\sum_j c_j \cdot [1 \otimes x_j] = [1 \otimes g_a]$ in $(S \otimes_R M)/N$ and $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,k$, is the whole of $S$.
--
--   This is the statement that a standard affine chart of a scheme representing the Grassmannian functor has coordinate ring generated over $R$ by the coordinates, in the basis $([1 \otimes x_i])_i$ of the universal quotient, of the classes of a spanning family of $M$ (EGA I, 9.7.4–9.7.5). It feeds the construction of the Plücker embedding, being used in the proof that a representing scheme admits a closed immersion into projective space.
